      <div class="research-content">
        <p class="research-number">01</p>

        <h2>Machine Learning and Scientific Understanding</h2>

        <p>
          Machine learning has become an increasingly important component
          of scientific research. Across disciplines ranging from climate
          science and biology to physics, machine-learning systems are now
          routinely used to generate predictions, uncover complex patterns,
          and support scientific investigation. Their growing role raises
          fundamental epistemological questions. Under what conditions can
          machine learning contribute to scientific knowledge? What kind of
          understanding can it provide? And how should scientists interpret
          both its successes and its failures?
        </p>

        <p>
          A particular focus of my work concerns the epistemic significance
          of predictive failure. While successful machine-learning predictions
          have received increasing philosophical attention, comparatively
          little has been said about situations in which these systems fail
          to meet expectations. I investigate how such failures can reveal
          hidden assumptions, expose limitations in existing theoretical
          frameworks, or point towards previously overlooked features of the
          phenomenon under investigation. In this sense, unsuccessful
          prediction is not simply a methodological setback, but can become
          an important source of scientific understanding.
        </p>

        <p>
          Methodologically, my work combines conceptual analysis with close
          engagement with contemporary machine-learning research and
          scientific case studies. Rather than treating machine learning as
          an abstract philosophical topic, I draw on examples from ongoing
          scientific practice to analyse how these systems contribute to
          scientific inquiry and how traditional epistemological concepts
          should be reconsidered in light of their increasing use.
        </p>
      </div>

    </article>

    <article class="research-area research-area-reversed">

      <div class="research-image">
  <img
    src="/images/mechanistic-interpretability.png"
  />
</div>

      <div class="research-content">
        <p class="research-number">02</p>

        <h2>Mechanistic Interpretability for Large Language Models</h2>

        <p>
          A second strand of my research concerns the use of mechanistic
          interpretability to investigate meaning, representation, and
          linguistic competence in large language models. The central
          question is not only whether language models produce fluent and
          successful linguistic behaviour, but whether their outputs depend
          on internal representations and procedures that are genuinely
          sensitive to conceptual information rather than to
          superficial statistical regularities. This requires distinguishing
          several dimensions of linguistic competence, including
          generalisation, compositionality, inferential and referential
          abilities, and the capacity to organise information into
          sufficiently structured internal models.
        </p>

        <p>
          Behavioural benchmarks alone are often unable to determine whether
          a model possesses such capacities, since the same performance may
          result from memorisation, data contamination, task-specific
          heuristics, or more general forms of competence. Mechanistic
          interpretability therefore provides an important source of evidence
          by examining how linguistic information is represented and causally
          used within the model. My research asks how such
          findings should bear on claims that language models understand
          meaning and which functional and mechanistic conditions should be
          satisfied before linguistic competence can responsibly be
          attributed to an artificial system.
        </p>
      </div>

     </article>

  <article class="research-area">

    <div class="research-image">
      <img
        src="/images/ml-climate-models.png"
